## Proof

The natural action of $\mathrm{SL}_k(\mathbb{Z})$ on $\mathbb{Z}^k$ is by left multiplication on column vectors. Thus $A \cdot e = Ae$ is precisely the first column of $A$. The stabilizer of $e$ is therefore

$$\mathrm{Stab}(e) = \{A \in \mathrm{SL}_k(\mathbb{Z}) : \text{first column of } A \text{ is } e\}.$$

**Structure of such matrices.** Any $A \in \mathrm{Stab}(e)$ has the block form

$$A = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & v \\ 0 & B \end{pmatrix},$$

where $v = (a_{1,2}, \dots, a_{1,k}) \in \mathbb{Z}^{k-1}$ is an arbitrary integer row vector and $B \in M_{k-1}(\mathbb{Z})$ is a $(k-1)\times(k-1)$ integer matrix.

**Determinant condition.** Expanding $\det(A)$ along the first column (which has entry $1$ in position $(1,1)$ and $0$ elsewhere) gives

$$\det(A) = 1 \cdot \det(B) = \det(B).$$

Hence $\det(A) = 1$ if and only if $\det(B) = 1$, i.e., $B \in \mathrm{SL}_{k-1}(\mathbb{Z})$.

**Bijection.** The map

$$\mathrm{Stab}(e) \longrightarrow \mathbb{Z}^{k-1} \times \mathrm{SL}_{k-1}(\mathbb{Z}), \qquad \begin{pmatrix} 1 & v \\ 0 & B \end{pmatrix} \mapsto (v, B)$$

is a bijection (its inverse sends $(v, B)$ to the block matrix above). Therefore

$$|\mathrm{Stab}(e)| = |\mathbb{Z}^{k-1}| \cdot |\mathrm{SL}_{k-1}(\mathbb{Z})|.$$

**Cardinality computation.** Since $k > 1$, we have $k - 1 \geq 1$, so $\mathbb{Z}^{k-1}$ is countably infinite: $|\mathbb{Z}^{k-1}| = \aleph_0$. The group $\mathrm{SL}_{k-1}(\mathbb{Z})$ is a subset of $\mathbb{Z}^{(k-1)^2}$ (all integer matrices of size $k-1$), which is countable, so $\mathrm{SL}_{k-1}(\mathbb{Z})$ is at most countable. It is infinite (it contains, for instance, all elementary matrices $E_{ij}(n)$ for $n \in \mathbb{Z}$, giving infinitely many distinct elements), so $|\mathrm{SL}_{k-1}(\mathbb{Z})| = \aleph_0$.

Therefore

$$|\mathrm{Stab}(e)| = \aleph_0 \cdot \aleph_0 = \aleph_0.$$

The stabilizer of $e$ in $\mathrm{SL}_k(\mathbb{Z})$ is **countably infinite**, i.e., its cardinality is $\boxed{\aleph_0}$.

